mathematical_discussion:
This Step took up A02's kernel-response question (#826, #827(4)) and Spirit #58's warning that a general W⁻ curvature jet cannot be represented by three Bianchi-IX radial functions. The question was whether the logarithmic Burns saving of Ch.5 (P/8π² = 1 − 9ε²log(1/ε) + O_R(ε²) on complex-hyperbolic collars) survives on a non-CH negative Kähler–Einstein background, given that the first-order response vanishes there because the perpendicular Burns form ζ lies in ker R⁺_p.

The structural insight came first. Ch.5's Burns core is exactly δ + ε(σ₁²+σ₂²) in Cartesian normal coordinates, since a_m² − t² = ε. The neck can therefore be written as g_bg + εk with k = k_B + free correctors. The logarithmic coefficient is then the z²y² Taylor coefficient of the overlap density, i.e. the homogeneous degree −4 part of |Ric°′_{g_bg}(k)|²√g integrated over S³, divided by 8π². Total t-derivatives cannot change this coefficient, because (2b−2a) vanishes at a = b = 2, so calibration choices are irrelevant. It depends only on the background jet to degree 4. Degree-2 correctors k_2 decouple, because Burns is Bach-flat and scalar-flat and the remaining pairing is a constant flux difference.

Representation theory fixes the form of the dependence. Burns is U(2)_ζ = U(1)_+×SU(2)_−-invariant, and W⁻ is spin 2 under SU(2)_−. Hence W⁻ can enter only through |W⁻_p|². There are no linear W⁻ terms, no cross terms with W⁺, s or Ric°, and ∇²W⁻ has no linear invariant. On every KE-type point the coefficient is therefore F_CH + κ|W⁻|²/s² with one universal κ. A corollary corrects A02's expectation: the Bianchi-IX diagonal squash modes are SU(2)_−-invariant and cannot couple to W⁻ at all. Bianchi-IX backgrounds have W⁻_p = 0, so only non-invariant angular correctors can respond to W⁻.

The decisive computation was built on exact Laurent extraction: Cauchy averages over complex radii with holomorphic JAX derivatives, which is exact to rounding error. The CH background reproduced Ch.5 exactly: F = 59/4 at c = 0 and −18 at c*. The CH optimum stays at −18 for general correctors up to angular degree 4, so non-diagonal correctors give no extra CH saving. On H²×H², where |W⁻|² = s²/24, the values are:
- Ch.5 diagonal ansatz: −12.4, so W⁻ costs 5.6 units;
- angular-degree-2 correctors: −15.9;
- angular-degree-4 correctors: exactly −18.000 (to 2e-11).
The W⁻ cost is thus a corrector artifact, and κ = 0 in the degree-4 class.

The resulting picture is that the logarithmic saving belongs to the R⁺-kernel orientation alone, not to the CH ball. To this order, every negative KE surface, at every point, gives P/8π² = 1 − ¼x²log(1/x) + O(x²), with x = Area(E)/π in Ric = −g. This removes the CH-region requirement from the Ch.5 strict sub-four construction: any fixed negative KE base with unramified covers gives limsup α/h < 4. It also strengthens A02's attainment chain: a KE bulk component, for the bubble's orientation, cannot carry a Burns bubble anywhere. This feeds Problems 1 and 3 (the k=1 dichotomy on X#CP̄²) and Problem 2 (the optimal constant is not tied to CH geometry).

The exact cancellation suggests a hidden identity: the W⁻ part of the Burns–background interaction lies in the range of the flat linearized Ric° on degree-0 tensors, with exactly matching quartic-jet terms. Proving it, and finding the explicit corrector in the SU(2)_− spin-2 isotypic part, would turn the numerics into a theorem.

Several things remain open:
- angular-degree-6 convergence (running); a larger class can only lower F;
- the rigorous general-background version of Ch.5's analytic factorization and O_R(ε²) remainder;
- non-KE W⁺ shapes with R⁺ ≤ 0, which need an Einstein 4-jet framework in which ΔW⁺(ζ,ζ) enters;
- non-Einstein bulk minimizers, which bring Ric°_p invariants.
Problem 4's symbolic AK jet engine and the near-KE family interpretation from Spirit #58 were not advanced in this Step.

