mathematical_discussion:
The Step-86 route named a scale-uniform C^1 estimate, ∂_{ζ_I}Λ_I=A+o(1), of Ozuch's raw normal obstruction as the decisive bottleneck at a one-node negative-KE endpoint. This Step shows the derivative is not needed. The actual Kähler geometry gives the normal behaviour exactly, both linearly and along a nonlinear secant. Only C^0 comparisons are left to prove.

The nonlinear secant comes from the Biquard–Rollin CSCK metrics, which vary the real Kronheimer period ζ_r (Spirit #110). Normalize a CSCK metric to s=4E. Then its whole Einstein tensor is Ric−Eg=Ric°=ρ0(·,J·), where ρ0 is the harmonic ASD (primitive (1,1)) representative of the class 2πc1−E[ω]. That class pairs with the vanishing cycle exactly: ∫_δρ0=−E·ζ_r. The defect is also topological: P=2‖ρ0‖²=−2(2πc1−E[ω])².

By an L²-minimization argument, the harmonic representative of a class that is ≈(Eζ_r/2)·PD(δ) concentrates on the Eguchi–Hanson L² form. The exact form of that argument is ‖α−ρ0‖²=2‖α⁺‖² for any closed α in the class. The EH form is d(c r^{-2}σ3). With ∫_Sθ=−2 it has ‖θ‖²=2 and |θ|=2√2a²/(πr⁴); a first attempt with mixed σ-conventions wrongly gave decay r^{-6}.

Hence the Einstein defect lies almost entirely along the single obstruction tensor θ∘I. In the period coordinate with an L²-unit test tensor, Λ_I=Eζ_r(1+o(1)) and P=Λ_I²(1+o(1)), so A=E, i.e. c0=1. This matches Ozuch's leading term R⁺(p)ζ with R⁺(p)=(s/4)ω̂⊗ω̂, which vanishes on the complex-period plane. The secant is exact because the defect is identically zero on the Kähler–Einstein sheet ζ_r=0, and its size is fixed by cohomology. No remainder needs to be differentiated.

The linear counterpart comes from Nagy (17): the Hermitian Einstein operator is Δ_d−2E. So every harmonic primitive (1,1) form gives an exact TT eigentensor θ∘J with Δ_E-eigenvalue −2E (−E for ½Δ_E). The Hermitian sector is uniformly ≥−2E, with multiplicity exactly h^{1,1}−1=44 for a quintic. The concentrated neck mode is one of these. In global units its eigenvalue is O(1); it is small only in core units.

The remaining approximate kernels (78 exterior + 2 smoothing directions) are, after global correction, the exact integrable Kodaira–Spencer kernel. The 39 locally trivial + 1 smoothing split was confirmed from Kloosterman §3 and the evaluation map. A uniform anti-Hermitian spectral gap is still a separate input (S).

Coverage then becomes a finite-dimensional topological statement. If the corrected CSCK metrics match Ozuch's parameters in one fixed reduction to relative C^0 accuracy below 1/2, a shell degree argument shows they fill the reduced Einstein-modulo-obstruction graph. Because ζ↦−ζ makes the parameter space nonorientable, the degree is taken on a marked lift, mod 2, or on a small ball. Every Einstein metric nearby then has ζ_r=0 and is Kähler–Einstein.

The length obstruction of Prop. 85.1 then excludes strict AKE in the complex orientation. Thm 83.2(b) excludes the reversed orientation, since that forces T_AK=12π²τ<0 for the quintic (τ=−35). On this route the Step-85 center and neck-trace theorems are no longer load-bearing. Classifying all nearby Einstein metrics is stronger than classifying AKE ones.

Independent verification sharpened what remains open. It is a single, well-posed analytic statement: a uniform, gauge-compatible weighted modulation comparison between the BR CSCK family and one fixed Ozuch reduction. Its parts are:
- the slice map m(p);
- the graph estimate in Ozuch's source norm r_D^{-2}C^α_β, including the regime |ζ_r|≪|ζ|;
- pairing stability;
- relative parameter matching, with recentering at g_v and Weyl-marking patching for both signs of ζ_r;
- the exact form of Ozuch's degeneration theorem (O1), not yet read in the original.
This replaces an unstructured C^1-in-scale estimate with a C^0 comparison of two existing gluing constructions. The latter is more plausible and more transferable.

The mechanism transfers formally to canonically polarized surfaces with any number of A1 nodes and no defect, to ADE points through the Cartan form, and to nodal del Pezzo surfaces. It does not transfer uniformly across Weyl walls (bubble trees), across different node scales, or to globally obstructed nodes. Obstructed nodes are therefore the natural habitat for non-Kähler Einstein metrics near KE orbifolds; there the live reduced equation is the anti-Hermitian one.

The broader goal is unchanged. A local endpoint theorem, even once completed, leaves open:
- Q1, with the full mixed bound D_W ≥ 4π²k+(8π²A/9)(1−√(1−3k/A))²;
- the Q2 tariff;
- Q3 attainment;
- closed Goldberg away from KE endpoints;
- collapse, cusp and diameter-divergent limits.
The CSCK identity P=−2(2πc1−E[ω])² realizes the same-metric competition 8π²k−P=4D_W−(S−32π²A)/4 on a concrete family.

