mathematical_discussion:
Step 94 ended with a specific question about the Wahl branch of Q1. The supplied forced Seiberg–Witten fields vanish to high order at the Wahl point. Does the coupled Einstein–SW Taylor recursion turn that order into a signed quantity that can enter the same-field endpoint deficit D2? Spirit #126 pointed out the structural fact that decides this. The Einstein metric is fixed and the spinor does not source it, so the recursion is governed by the flat principal block L0 = D0 ⊕ (d* ⊕ √2 d+). This step turned that observation into a complete theorem.

Everything rests on a degree count. In normal coordinates with a synchronous frame, the metric corrections have degree ≥2 and the connection terms degree ≥1. The Clifford term a·Φ and the quadratic q(Φ) involve only coefficients of degree below k, because Φ starts at degree ℓ and an invariant a has no constant term. So the degree-k unknowns appear in degree k−1 only through L0. L0 is surjective on homogeneous polynomials with an equivariant, reality-preserving right inverse: D0D0* = −Δ/4, 2d+d* = Δ on self-dual forms, and the Fischer inverse of the Laplacian is O(4)-equivariant.

Theorem 95.1 follows. For every Γ-invariant metric jet, Einstein or not, every permitted leading monogenic term, every amplitude and every free ASD determinant curvature extend to a formal solution of every order, which is Borel-smooth to infinite order. No finite-jet pairing can contribute to D2. The Einstein condition enters only as source coefficients. The marked character fixes a minimal degree and nothing scale-independent beyond that.

This matters because it settles finite-order compatibility once for all orders, instead of through a sequence of curvature calculations. It also locates the remaining Wahl question precisely. Any obstruction must be global. The natural global continuation, frequency and doubling estimates from the vanishing order, makes almost the whole harmonic ball depleted. But it pays only scalar slack of size about (λR²)²/n²: the orbifold volume factor 1/n² suppresses it, and it sits exactly at the unresolved scale λr0² from Step 93. Spirit #125 showed that the mixed Einstein edge absorbs scalar slack of this kind throughout 0<k<A/3.

The judgment is therefore about research value, not a theorem. The Wahl line has given real capabilities:
- lossless transfer of the supplied field;
- index transfer;
- marked complex realization;
- forced characters;
- the order formula;
- formal freedom.

It has no present mechanism for Q1. It would reopen only with a global datum that keeps λR² bounded below and pairs the depleted region with W+ or the harmonic projection in D2.

For reallocation I recorded one elementary structural fact for Q4. A closed negative Einstein metric of locally homogeneous cohomogeneity one is impossible. With Bianchi VIII principal orbits, χ(M) is the sum of χ over the hyperbolic bolts, so χ(M) < 0, and Hitchin–Thorpe excludes Einstein. The flat-type Bianchi classes give χ = 0, which forces a flat metric. Bianchi IX is excluded by Bochner. So the Step-57 strict AKE model cannot be closed up by a second bolt. A closed Goldberg counterexample would have to be genuinely inhomogeneous, for example by compactifying or gluing the ACH end. That needs an AKE deformation theory, because the system of an Einstein metric plus a constant-length harmonic self-dual form is overdetermined. The alternative is an integrability proof that uses global structure.

The central open problems are unchanged: Q1 existence for 0<k<A/3, Q2 the optimal unrestricted coefficient and P*, Q3 attainment with a nonzero Bach tensor, and Q4 closed Goldberg.
